-- comments
1:1 plain -- A `<` after a token that can end an operand is the comparison, exactly as
2:1 plain -- before markup existed (frontend.md §9.3): after a name (`a <b`), after an
3:1 plain -- argument (`f a <b`), after `)` (`(x) <y`), after a string's closing quote,
4:1 plain -- and inside `${…}`, where `<` is always the operator. `(<)` is still the
5:1 plain -- operator function: its `<` is followed by `)`, not a letter.
